ritz for Debian
===============

Upstream source
---------------
Ritz has no releases and no tarball of its own: it lives in the ritz/
subdirectory of the Zino suite's git repository, which this packaging
repository carries as its upstream branch. The orig tarball is therefore the
whole Zino tree, and only the ritz/ subtree is used at build time.

Because upstream never bumps its version (the top-level Makefile has said
VERSION=1.0 since the 1990s), the package uses snapshot versions of the form
1.0+gitYYYYMMDD.<short-sha>, taken from the upstream commit the orig tarball
was exported from.

How the binary is built
-----------------------
There is no real upstream build step: the `ritz` program is the Tcl sources
concatenated into one wish script. Upstream's ritz/Makefile does this with
pmake and a site-specific Makefile.inc describing a NORDUnet host, which is
not usable here, so debian/rules reproduces the same recipe and applies four
adaptations to the generated script:

  * the shebang points at Debian's /usr/bin/wish;
  * "namespace import ::Tnm::*" is injected, because this Tnm (the FlightAware
    scotty fork) namespaces its commands, unlike the 1998-era Tnm ritz was
    written against;
  * "mib load rfc1213.mib" becomes "mib load RFC1213-MIB"; the modern Tnm
    loads MIBs by module name, and RFC1213-MIB is already a core MIB;
  * the %SHA% auth helper (NetBSD's sha1) is replaced by coreutils sha1sum.
    Upstream substitutes %SHA% through its own mk-subst.awk mechanism, which
    only knows how to insert a command path; sha1sum prints a trailing
    filename field, so the call needs an extra [lindex] that the upstream
    mechanism cannot express.

version.tcl is generated too. Upstream's top-level Makefile writes it and does
not track it in git, so debian/rules writes it from the changelog's upstream
version. This means the About box names the exact snapshot the binary was
built from.

Because all of this touches only generated artifacts (removed again by
dh_auto_clean), there are no quilt patches.

Dependencies
------------
ritz needs tk (for wish), tix (widgets) and tcl-tnm (>= 3.1.3), the companion
package built from the scotty source in the scotty-debian repository.

The router menu shells out to the host: "telnet" opens `xterm -e telnet` and
the traceroute entry pipes from `traceroute`. Those are Depends rather than
Recommends, because the menu entries are not optional extras but part of what
operators use ritz for.

 -- Morten Brekkevold <morten.brekkevold@sikt.no>
